---
import Page from "../components/Page.astro";

const formulas = [
	["TTM", "Flows (revenue, earnings, cash flow) summed over the last four reported quarters; balance sheet items from the last quarter. When four quarters are not available, the last fiscal year."],
	["Enterprise value", "Market capitalisation + total debt − cash − short-term investments."],
	["P/E", "Price ÷ diluted EPS. Left blank when EPS is zero or negative, because a P/E on losses means nothing."],
	["EV/EBITDA, EV/Sales, EV/EBIT", "Enterprise value ÷ the trailing figure. Blank when the figure is not positive."],
	["P/FCF, FCF yield", "Market capitalisation ÷ free cash flow, and its inverse. Free cash flow = operating cash flow − capital expenditure."],
	["ROE", "Net income ÷ average shareholders' equity of the period and the one before. Blank when equity is not positive."],
	["ROIC", "Operating income × (1 − effective tax rate) ÷ (equity + debt − cash). The effective rate is income tax ÷ pre-tax income, replaced by the 21% US statutory rate when it falls outside 0–50%."],
	["Net debt / EBITDA", "Negative values mean net cash."],
	["Historical multiples", "Each fiscal year's multiple at the closing price of its fiscal year end (or the last session before it), with that year's diluted share count and balance sheet."],
	["Forward multiples", "Today's price (or enterprise value) ÷ the analysts' average estimate for each coming fiscal year. The P/E range uses the highest and lowest EPS estimates. PEG = next year's forward P/E ÷ (consensus EPS CAGR × 100)."],
	["RSI", "Wilder's 14-day Relative Strength Index."],
	["MACD", "12- and 26-day exponential averages, 9-day signal line."],
	["Bollinger bands", "20-day average ± 2 standard deviations."],
	["Beta", "Covariance of daily returns with the S&P 500 (SPY) over the last year ÷ the variance of SPY's."],
	["Swing levels", "Lows and highs that are the extreme of five sessions on each side, over the last six months, grouped when within 1.5% of each other."],
];
---

<Page title="Method" description="Where the numbers come from and how each one is computed.">
	<div class="max-w-3xl">
		<h1 class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-stone-900">Sources and formulas</h1>
		<p class="mt-4 text-stone-600">
			Every figure on the site is computed by code from the data below, in the same way for every company. The AI reading is
			written from those figures and only from them.
		</p>

		<h2 class="mt-10 font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-stone-900">Sources</h2>
		<ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-stone-600">
			<li><strong class="text-stone-800">Financial Modeling Prep</strong>: company profile, income statement, balance sheet and cash flow (annual and quarterly), daily prices adjusted for splits and dividends, and the analysts' consensus estimates.</li>
			<li><strong class="text-stone-800">SEC EDGAR</strong>: the list of US-listed companies behind the search, and the XBRL facts of 10-K filings as a fallback for the annual statements.</li>
			<li><strong class="text-stone-800">Claude (Anthropic)</strong>: the AI reading.</li>
		</ul>
		<p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-500">A report is kept for twelve hours; an AI reading for the day. Figures follow each company's fiscal year, which does not always end in December.</p>

		<h2 class="mt-10 font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-stone-900">Formulas</h2>
		<dl class="mt-3 divide-y divide-stone-200 rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 bg-white">
			{formulas.map(([term, text]) => (
				<div class="grid gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[12rem_1fr] sm:gap-4">
					<dt class="font-medium text-stone-900">{term}</dt>
					<dd class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-stone-600">{text}</dd>
				</div>
			))}
		</dl>

		<h2 class="mt-10 font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-stone-900">The AI reading</h2>
		<p class="mt-3 text-stone-600">
			The model receives a compact document with the figures of the page (no chart series) and writes what they show: growth,
			profitability, balance sheet, valuation against its own history and the consensus, and price behaviour, plus a few points
			worth checking. It is instructed to use only those numbers, to describe rather than advise, not to forecast prices and not to
			call a stock cheap or expensive. It can still be wrong: the figures on the page are the reference.
		</p>

		<h2 class="mt-10 font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-stone-900">What this is not</h2>
		<p class="mt-3 text-stone-600">
			This site describes numbers. It does not recommend buying, selling or holding any security, does not set price targets and
			does not forecast returns. Forward multiples are the published consensus of the analysts at today's price, which the
			company may or may not meet. Technical indicators describe past prices. Data from third parties may be delayed or contain
			errors.
		</p>
	</div>
</Page>
